texto:
So I've been rewriting our little config parser in Rust this week, finally got tired of segfaults in the old C version, no offense to C, and I hit a borrow checker error that's been staring me down for like an hour now. Here's roughly the shape of the code:

fn process(items: &mut Vec<Item>) {
    let first = &items[0];
    items.push(Item::default());
    println!("{}", first.name);
}

And rustc yells at me with something like `cannot borrow items as mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable`, pointing at the push call. I get why conceptually, a push could reallocate the vector and invalidate that reference, but I don't get why the compiler can't just tell that first isn't used after the push, since println comes after. Is this one of those cases where non-lexical lifetimes just aren't smart enough yet, or am I missing something more fundamental about how references interact with methods that might reallocate? Also open to the idiomatic fix, clone the name out early, restructure so the borrow ends before the mutation, something else entirely?
